    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, independent since 1 January 1804 as the only state established by a slave revolt, paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> 150 million francs (reduced to 90 million in 1838) for recognition; by 1900 debt repayment took 80% of government spending, and the debt was not cleared until 1947.</li><li>US Marines occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> from July 1915 to 1934, imposing a constitution that allowed foreign land ownership and building roads by forced labour; the Duvaliers (1957–1986) ruled by terror through the Tontons Macoutes, killing an estimated 40,000 to 60,000 people.</li><li>The earthquake of 12 January 2010 killed between 160,000 and 316,000 people; since the assassination of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise">Jovenel Moïse</a> on 7 July 2021 the presidency has been vacant, gangs control most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> and no elected official remains.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The constitution written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> under US occupation in 1915, which for the first time allowed foreigners to own land in the country, was drafted by the future US president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

