    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Dessalines" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Jacques_Dessalines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis Rigaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, was reclaimed in 1804 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> for the state the enslaved people of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> had won. The revolution begun in August 1791 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>, the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1793 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition of 1802, ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vertieres">Battle of Vertières</a> on 18 November 1803 and in the declaration of independence of 1 January 1804: the first state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to abolish slavery and the only one established by a slave revolt. Dessalines, Emperor Jacques I, had 3,000 to 5,000 remaining whites killed and was assassinated in 1806; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>'s northern kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a>'s southern republic were reunited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer">Jean-Pierre Boyer</a> in 1820, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in 1822.</p><p>In 1825, under the guns of a French fleet, Boyer bought recognition for 150 million francs. He fell in 1843, the Dominicans broke away on 27 February 1844, and Haiti's armies failed to retake the east in 1845 and 1849; General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faustin-soulouque">Faustin Soulouque</a>, president from 1847, made himself emperor and launched the campaigns of 1849 and 1855. Their losses and sacrifices without result provoked a revolution led by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fabre-geffrard">Fabre Geffrard</a> in 1858, who defeated the army and took most of the country in December. Soulouque abdicated on 15 January 1859 and went into exile, and Geffrard became president of the restored republic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou.jpg" alt="Haitian Vodou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoodooValris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sam Fentress, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> occupies the mountainous western three-eighths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, 27,750 square kilometres, and with about 11.4 million people is the most populous country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Nearly all speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-creole">Haitian Creole</a>, French the educated 42%; 55% were Catholic and 29% Protestant in 2018, while 50–80% keep some element of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a>, recognised by the state in 2003. The colonial order survived as a social one: the mulatto elite, 5% of the population, has kept its pre-eminence in politics, economy and culture. Haiti is the only country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> classified as least developed: three-quarters of its people live on US$2 or less a day, 30% in extreme poverty in 2021, the informal economy gives 90% of employment, and 75% of households lack running water.</p><p>Agriculture employs 40–50% of Haitians; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, mangoes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and vetiver, of which Haiti grows half the world's supply for perfume, are exported, but the country imports half its food and 80% of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Remittances from the diaspora in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> equal a fifth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, five times export earnings, and by 2004 four in five Haitian graduates lived abroad. Aid has flowed since 1990, more than US$4 billion by 2003 and US$13.34 billion earmarked after the 2010 earthquake, with mixed results: the Caracol industrial park opened in 2012 with 65,000 promised jobs had created 5,000 and displaced 3,500 farmers a decade later.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier.jpg" alt="François Duvalier" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier">François Duvalier</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ois_Duvalier_Presidential_Portrait_(1957).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Haitian army inherited the glory of 1803, but in the republic it made and broke presidents: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> has suffered 32 coups since independence. In July 1915, after President Vilbrun Guillaume Sam executed 167 political prisoners and was lynched, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> sent in the Marines; they took the capital, its banks and customs house within days. The Cacos under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne-peralte">Charlemagne Péralte</a> fought them until his capture and execution in 1919, and the Marine commandant counted 2,250 or 3,250 Haitians killed in twenty months of unrest. The occupation ended in 1934.</p><p>In 1937 Dominican troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a> killed 20,000–30,000 Haitians on the border in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsley-massacre">Parsley Massacre</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier">François Duvalier</a>, distrusting an army he purged repeatedly, built the Tontons Macoutes, a private militia that terrorised the population; the 1964 rising at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremie">Jérémie</a> was crushed with public executions. The army overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bertrand-aristide">Jean-Bertrand Aristide</a> in September 1991 under Raoul Cédras, and in September 1994 20,000 US troops under Operation Uphold Democracy restored him. The Armed Forces were disbanded in 1995, a National Police took their place, and the army was reconstituted only in 2017, some 2,000 strong by 2023. Since 2020 the state has lost its monopoly of force: gangs held about 80% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> by late 2023, killed 4,789 people and kidnapped 2,490 that year, and neither the police, bled by desertion, nor the Kenyan-led Multinational Security Support Mission has restored public safety.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise.jpg" alt="Jovenel Moïse" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise">Jovenel Moïse</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jovenel_Mo%C3%AFse_in_Taiwan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">總統府, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic of 1859 was presidential and unstable. Under the US occupation (1915–1934) a client president was installed within weeks and a constitution drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> permitted foreign ownership of land for the first time; an American financial adviser ran the budget until 1941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-duvalier">François Duvalier</a>, elected in September 1957, proclaimed himself President for Life in 1964 and ruled through the Tontons Macoutes; his son Jean-Claude succeeded him in 1971 and fled in February 1986. Some 40,000 to 60,000 Haitians were killed under the two Duvaliers.</p><p>The Constitution of 1987, amended in 2012, made a semi-presidential republic: a president elected for five years without consecutive terms, a prime minister from the majority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-bulgaria">National Assembly</a>, a two-chamber National Assembly and a modified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>. It has rarely worked as written. Elections in 1987 were aborted by shootings, those of 1988 drew 4% of voters, Aristide was elected in 1990 and overthrown in 1991, re-elected in 2000 with 92% on an opposition boycott and driven out in 2004; Michel Martelly left in 2016 without a successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise">Jovenel Moïse</a> won on a 17% turnout and was assassinated in 2021. Since 2023 no elected official remains: a Transitional Presidential Council governed from 25 April 2024 until its mandate expired on 7 February 2026, leaving acting prime minister Alix Didier Fils-Aimé as sole executive with elections postponed indefinitely.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere.jpg" alt="Citadelle Laferrière" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rémi Kaupp, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>'s weight in history is out of all proportion to its size. Its revolution ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the richest plantation colony on earth, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, who then sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and so frightened the slave-holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> that recognition waited until 1862. Pétion's republic gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> the arms and money that let him liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, and Haiti ruled the whole island from 1822 to 1844, ending slavery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. The ransom paid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, estimated to have cost between $21 billion and $115 billion in lost growth, made Haiti the first post-colonial state crippled by debt; in 2013 its government called on European governments to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> for slavery.</p><p>The republic was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and, on 27 September 1945, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. Its diaspora in the United States, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> carried its culture abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-creole">Haitian Creole</a>, official beside French, has had a literature of its own since Franketienne's Dezafi (1975), and writers from Jacques Roumain to Edwidge Danticat and Dany Laferrière and the music of compas and Rara are known across the world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a>, the largest fortress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sans-souci-palace">Sans-Souci Palace</a> were inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 1982. Haiti's disasters have also shaped the world's institutions: the UN's cholera, brought in 2010 by its own peacekeepers and killing some 10,000, drew an apology in 2016 but no admission of fault.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>'s recent decades have been a descent. After the Duvaliers' flight in 1986 came aborted and fraudulent elections, Aristide's election and overthrow in 1990–91, his restoration by US troops in 1994, the contested election of 2000 and the revolt that forced him out again in 2004. Then nature struck a broken state: on 12 January 2010 a magnitude-7.0 earthquake killed between 160,000 and 316,000 people and left up to 1.6 million homeless; cholera from a UN base then killed some 10,000. Hurricane Matthew delayed the elections of 2016, won on a 17% turnout by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise">Jovenel Moïse</a>.</p><p>Protests against fuel prices from July 2018 became demands for Moïse's resignation; on 7 July 2021 he was assassinated at his residence, and a second great earthquake struck on 14 August. By March 2022 Haiti had no president, no parliamentary quorum and no working high court, and the gang war begun in 2020 has consumed the country: armed groups held an estimated 80% of the capital by late 2023, killed 4,789 people, kidnapped 2,490 and displaced 362,000 that year; over 2.7 million live under gang control and more than 1.3 million have been displaced, and in March 2024 nearly half the population faced acute food insecurity. Ariel Henry, kept out of the country by gangs, resigned; the Transitional Presidential Council ruled from 25 April 2024 to 7 February 2026 without an election. With no elected official left, Haiti has been described as a failed state in virtual anarchy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, restored in 1859 after Soulouque's empire, inherited the pride and the debts of the only nation born of a slave revolt. Coups, the US occupation of 1915–1934, the Duvalier terror and the earthquake of 2010 wore down the state, and since the assassination of President Moïse in 2021 gangs rule most of the capital and no elected official remains.</em></p>

